Barrier-pooled virtual approximation: invariant polynomials, format version 2
============================================================================

New NLO exports enable #UsePooledVirtual in Cards/FKS_params.dat. Old cards
without that key retain the previous default. Set it false to disable pooling
and use the existing UsePolyVirtual selection or MINT marginals. Restart from
grid setup after changing the mode or physics inputs; never reuse an old
unweighting envelope with a changed model.

Workflow and normalization
--------------------------
Workers independently record virtuals already evaluated during MC@NLO step 0
or fixed-order NLO step 1. Only after the entire stage finishes does the
controller call train_pool(job_dirs, output_model, report_path, job_groups).
It pools integration channels and split replicas within the same actual Born
subprocess and fits each coupling order separately. There is no communication
among workers during a stage. Frozen models are copied into subsequent job
directories, including cluster transfers. Controller resumes verify checksums.

job_groups maps each directory to its physical integration-channel batch.
Split replicas share an attempted-point denominator. Different batches have
separate denominators. Without this mapping, each directory is its own group.
The arbitrary fixed-order replica multiplier WGT_MULT is removed at capture;
all physical integration, PDF, bias and native helicity factors remain.

B is the original native Born carrier used for the virtual correction. V is
the sampled-helicity estimator, with its existing proposal weight included.
A is the contemporaneous MINT approximation, retained for reconstruction and
diagnostics. V is reconstructed as probability*residual+A, before bookkeeping
can clear a Born entry with zero residual. No additional helicity factor is
applied. All orders of a phase-space observation share its sample ID and fold.
Neither V nor B is divided by the per-point Born or by the loop probability
in the file. A training row has statistical weight

  1 / (accepted attempted points in its integration group * loop probability).

Failed cuts and unevaluated virtuals remain in the attempted-point denominator.
Rejected and uncommitted iterations are discarded. A monotonically increasing
iteration ordinal remains unique even when native MINT restarts its counter.
Born-spreading calibration restarts discard pre-calibration observations.

Model and conservative selection
--------------------------------
The candidate is A_new=(1-lambda) A_MINT + lambda B P(z). P is linear or
quadratic, including all cross terms. The invariant coordinate catalog is:
  log(shat/muR^2),
  log(max(abs(2*pi.pj)/shat,1e-15)) for all i<j in native Born order,
  log(shat/m^2) for distinct positive external masses in ascending order.
The renormalization scale is the actual runtime scale. Coordinates do not
require a particular spin, colour representation, or particle identity.
This is a generic fitting basis, not an exact formula for arbitrary virtuals.

Scalers use fitting rows only and map nonconstant coordinates to [-1,1].
Constants and negligible columns are removed. Regression fits weighted V
through V-B*P without per-point Born division. Born/target/column normalization
keeps the small linear systems well scaled. The fixed candidate scan is:
  degree 1 or 2; ridge 1e-4,0.01,1; Huber or squared loss;
  blend 0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1. Huber IRLS has eight iterations and a fixed
  residual threshold of 0.25 times the weighted mean absolute target scale.

Three-fold CV is stratified by job/channel/iteration, keeping coupling orders
of the same point together. Each validation baseline is a frozen native MINT
reconstruction from that same fold complement, including the recorded 4-to-8
bin schedule. The stored causal A is NOT the validation baseline. Worker and
channel histories remain distinct, as in the native grids. All sampled rows,
including exact zero residuals, enter the MINT reconstruction.

A candidate must reduce weighted L1 by more than both 2% and twice a
conservative paired standard-error estimate. Its aggregate RMS must not
increase; no individual fold may increase RMS by more than 5%. A linear
candidate within one paired error estimate of the best L1 is preferred over
quadratic complexity. The error estimate is sqrt(sum((w_i*delta_i)^2)), with
normalized weights and paired absolute-residual differences. It prevents one
large observed gain from establishing significance by itself. These tuning
diagnostics are not independent performance measurements.

At least 60 observations and ten observations in every validation fold are
required. Each fitting fold must have at least five rows per candidate
coefficient; at most 512 coefficients are allowed. Higher multiplicities or
small samples therefore use a linear model or MINT automatically. Zero
Born/virtual orders, unsuccessful fits or no admissible improvement produce an
explicit zero-blend MINT fallback. Without NumPy on the controller, no model is
installed: ordinary MINT marginals and adaptive virtual-call fractions remain
active, including on later split jobs and resumes. Resuming a previously fitted
model does not need NumPy; workers evaluate it in Fortran and retain its frozen
event envelope. Captures and models use format version 2.
Earlier experimental formats are rejected; restart their integration from
grid setup before using this implementation.

Exact correction, boundaries and call budget
-------------------------------------------
The original helicity sampler and RNG are unchanged. The estimator remains
A + I*(V_h-A)/p with the actual positive loop-call probability p. Models are
frozen at the barrier. The first-stage channel probabilities are retained
while applying them, so better residuals do not automatically lower the loop
budget. Native driver policies can still explicitly require exact loops.

Features clamp to their training boundaries. If any coordinate of an active
model lies more than 10% of its training range beyond a boundary, that order
uses MINT and the point requests an exact virtual (p=1). A constant coordinate
has tolerance 1e-10*max(1,abs(value)). Invalid coordinates in apply mode also
request an exact loop. The point's actual probability is used for residual
normalization, exact-V reconstruction and virtual-envelope bookkeeping.
Folded evaluations reuse the same approximation and sampled residual.
No positivity clipping or deletion of negative weights is performed.

The collection stage itself cannot benefit from the model. Short fixed-order
runs ending at step 1 only train; later integration stages consume the fit.
A better residual does not guarantee fewer negative unweighted events or a
net timing gain. Full-event tests and total fitting/inference overhead matter.

Version-2 files
---------------
virtual_pool_samples.dat has five header lines:
  VIRTUAL_POOL_SAMPLES 2
  5 nf norders nlegs nx
  pdg(1:nlegs)
  mass(1:nlegs)
  incoming(1:nlegs)

nx is the number of Born MINT random coordinates (ndim-3). Exactly two legs
are incoming. Each subsequent row is:
  sampleid channel iteration order bins probability B V A features(1:nf) x(1:nx)

bins is 4 or 8. All numerical fields are finite, x is in [0,1], and probability
is in (0,1]. Metadata and native Born ordering must match across workers.

virtual_pool_iterations.dat uses the iteration ledger format:
  iteration accepted attemptedpoints
where accepted is 0 or 1. Missing ledgers contribute no uncommitted rows.

virtual_pool_model.dat has the same header layout, beginning with
VIRTUAL_POOL_MODEL 2. For each coupling order it then stores:
  order lambda born_scale target_scale nterms degree
  lower(1:nf)
  upper(1:nf)
  a b coefficient             [one row per term]

Feature indices are one-based; index zero means the constant 1. Thus (0,0)
is a constant, (a,0) a linear term, and (a,b) a quadratic product. The raw
Born multiplier is target_scale/born_scale times the polynomial sum.
Degree is 0,1,2; zero blend reproduces MINT exactly. Constant boundary scaling,
out-of-domain fallback and polynomial ordering agree between Python/Fortran.

Models and JSON reports are written atomically. Per-job mode markers remain
0=inactive,1=collect,2=apply. A no-header fallback needs no model. Reports give
actual point counts, candidate diagnostics, selected models and fit wall time.
A subprocess manifest binds the model checksum to the integration workflow.

Implementation
--------------
virtual_pool.py: accepted-record pooling, serialization and diagnostics.
virtual_pool_polynomial.py: regression, matched MINT replay and CV selection.
virtual_pooled.f90: generic coordinates, capture and frozen native inference.
